## What the three implement rounds actually bought

Every non-passing round found a **fail-open on the push path** — a case where the
audit reported clean on something it was supposed to catch. That is worth stating
plainly, because this bead's job was to *shrink* a safety check, and shrinking a
safety check is exactly when fail-opens get written.

| Round | Fail-open |
|---|---|
| r1 | a run ref with no readable `manifest.json` exited **0, clean**, on a hard check — a `pipefail` interaction where `\|\| echo -1` appended a second line to a sentinel that had already been printed |
| r1 | `git ls-remote` failure swallowed by `\|\| true`, turning an unreachable origin into a repo-wide pile of "unpushed" gaps and exit 1 instead of exit 2 |
| r2 | a `case` with no default arm, so any unexpected value passed |
| r2 | `gates` counted with `len()` and no type check, so `{"gates":"xx"}` passed |
| r2 | `mapfile < <(python3 …)` discarding the producer's exit status, so a truncated window read as clean |
| r2 | `for-each-ref` failure making check (d) pass vacuously |

The first was found independently by both seats and reproduced before fixing. The
902-line predecessor handled it correctly, so it was a regression this bead
introduced — and the new test had **no** bad-manifest coverage at all, which is
why it survived my own review. Three of the r2 four were inherited from the
predecessor; inherited is not acceptable in a rewrite whose subject is a check
that must not fail open.

## Negative controls

Each targets a branch whose breakage is observable in the assertion being
watched — the lesson from bead `.3`, where a control aimed at a masked branch
proved nothing. Run and reverted:

| Control | Result |
|---|---|
| local `refs/etude/runs/*` absent from origin | exit 1, `[unpushed-ref]` |
| same tree, no gap | exit 0 — so the above is attributable |
| closed bead with no run ref | exit 1, `[missing-run]` |
| run ref with zero gates | exit 1, `[gateless-run]` |
| **run ref with no `manifest.json`** | exit 1, `[bad-manifest]` — this is the r1 fail-open |
| 3 uncovered beads, hard checks clean | exit 0 **with** `WARN [cadence-overdue]` |
| unknown flag / `--last 0` / malformed bd output | exit 2 |
| unreachable origin | exit 2, and no `unpushed-ref` lines |
| origin advanced from a separate clone | `[diverged-ref]`, and no "push it" instruction |

**The r1 regression was itself negative-controlled**: reinstating the exact
broken line made the suite report 26 passed / 2 failed; restoring returned 28/28
(now 34/34). A regression that has never been seen to fail is a guess.

## Known limitations, disclosed

- **Check (c) measures retro REFS while lanes write retro MARKDOWN.** It is
  WARN-only for that reason: a ratchet firing on the wrong evidence trains people
  to bypass it. Filed as `etude-3xt`.
- **Nothing enforces the 7-key retro-meta sidecar any more.** Check (f) was its
  only enforcement, and `scripts/retro-meta-index.sh` still consumes those
  sidecars, so its coverage now degrades silently. Filed as `etude-k47`;
  `docs/retro.md` and `retro-ledger.md` both say so outright rather than implying
  the guard still exists.
- **Nothing forces `etude gate` to be used.** Checks (a) and (b) DETECT a
  supervisor who skipped the model; they do not prevent it. That gap is what
  justified keeping them, and it is not closed by this bead.
- **`make dogfood-audit` still depends on network reachability**, since check (d)
  calls `git ls-remote`. An unreachable origin is now correctly an exit 2 rather
  than a false gap, but it does mean the audit cannot run fully offline.
